>What should the game do if the imported file contains something that isn't
>compiled in the game that loads the file? One possibility would be to
>totally ignore the unsupported thing, another would be to replace it with
>something else close enough that IS supported. In the latter case, there
>should be some pointer in the optional / additional item structure that
>would tell what it can be replaced with. 
>Like, if some patch adds a monster "lion", it could contain information
>that directs an unpatched game to replace it with a tiger.
>And the vacuum cleaner could be replaced with a bag of holding or a sack
>in an unpatched game.

If a lion with 35hp (40hp max) is replaced by a tiger (32hp max), how
many hitpoints does it have? 32? 35? 35*(32/40)? If a "holy spear of
stoning" is transformed into a cockatrice corpse, will it stone the
player? What happens if the new monster can use weapons or wear armour,
and the monster to replace it cannot? If tigers are genocided, what
happens to the lion; and what would happen to a player polymorphed into a
lion? 

There are probably many, many more special cases that all have to be
thought about for this to work. And I don't think it is worth it, because
such a scheme would probably not be used by many people:

It is important to distinguish two aspects. On the one hand, we have
people who played on an unpatched version and want to used saved games
and bones files with a patched version. In this context, the issue does
not arise, because a patch is likely not to delete monsters and the
players will keep playing the patched version. The idea of placeholders
for objects and monsters would work in this setting without additional
code.

On the other hand, people might want to transfer saved games or bones
files between games with different sets of patches, /in both directions/.
Only in this case the problems that you mention arise, and only for
objects and monsters, not for interface flags.

So there is a very limited applicability for your proposal, and it would
probably not be worth deciding on aedequate placeholders for new entities
and programming the necessary infrastructure for this to work.

